4:     # ── eviction ────────────────────────────────────────────\n65: \n66:     def evict_idle(self) -> Connection | None:\n67:         \"\"\"Remove the least-recently-used idle connection.\"\"\"\n68:         if not self.connections:\n69:             return None\n70: \n71:         now = time.time()\n72:         evicted = min(self.connections, key=lambda c: c.last_used_at)\n73:         self.connections.remove(evicted)\n74:         idle_seconds = now - evicted.last_used_at\n75:         orgops.metrics.emit(\"pool.connection.evicted\", idle_seconds)\n76:         return evicted connection with the oldest last_used_at (LRU order)\n        lru_idx = min(range(len(self.connections)),\n                      key=lambda i: self.connections[i].last_used_at)\n        evicted = self.connections.pop(lru_idx)\n\n        idle_seconds = now - evicted.last_used_at\n        orgops.metrics.emit(\"pool.connection.evicted\", idle_seconds)\n        return evicted\n```\n\nThe key changes are: importing orgops.metrics at the method level, using `min()` with a lambda to find the connection index with the smallest `last_used_at` value instead of always popping index 0, and emitting the eviction metric with the idle duration. after the docstring for clarity\n2. Changed from `self.connections.pop(0)` to finding the actual LRU connection using `min()` with a lambda that checks `last_used_at` timestamps\n3. Added the metrics emission call to track idle time when evicting\n4. The docstring documents the bug fix - explaining that the original code was removing FIFO connections instead of truly least-recently-used ones connections:\\n            return None\\n\\n        now = time.time()\\n\\n        # Find the connection with the oldest last_used_at (LRU)\\n        lru_idx = min(range(len(self.connections)),\\n                      key=lambda i: self.connections[i].last_used_at)\\n        evicted = self.connections.pop(lru_idx)\\n\\n        idle_seconds = now - evicted.last_used_at\\n        orgops